# EXAMPLE lane declaration (synthetic Testburg vocabulary — see
# cabinet/fixtures/testburg/README.md; everything here is fiction). Ships in
# the egg so a fresh hatch has a concrete model of a lane-context file:
#   1. copy to instance/config/contexts/<your-lane>.yml
#   2. set `slug:` to your lane (the FILENAME minus .yml must equal the slug —
#      the hook-side lane cache is built from these filenames)
#   3. commit — declaration alone makes the slug enumerable; activation is a
#      separate explicit Captain step (see _default.yml).
#
# The shared lane resolvers enumerate instance/config/contexts/*.yml by each
# file's first top-level `slug:` scalar: framework/env.py lanes(),
# cabinet/scripts/lib/lanes.sh cabinet_lanes, and the dashboard's
# declaredLanes(). This .example file matches no *.yml glob, so it is never a
# live lane. NOTE (the active-flag rule): `active:` is an activation gate,
# NOT a resolver filter — a declared lane enumerates even while
# `active: false`, so officer/world derivations never silently drop a
# declared-but-not-yet-activated lane.
slug: bakery-site
name: Bakery Site
capacity: work
description: |
  Ada Testburg's bakery-site lane (synthetic example). The storefront web
  product of the Testburg bakery. A product lane pairs 1:1 with
  instance/config/projects/<slug>.yml — copy projects/_template.yml and
  fill in the product block for your lane.
active: false
